{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: round seven, and it is small — one comment paragraph and an artifact refile. Both blockers are truthfulness and both are mine.\n\nTHE CODE SENTENCE CONTRADICTS ITSELF IN ONE PARAGRAPH. I read it at the head to confirm. It names absolute include targets as admitted, which is right and which I measured, and then two sentences later says the bound stops that scope reaching outside itself. An absolute include is exactly that. Codex's replacement is both accurate and shorter.\n\nTHE ARTIFACT DEFECT IS THE ONE I WANT TO NAME PROPERLY, because it is the sixth instance of one habit and this time the shape is clearer than before. I did qualify the closed-environment claim to Unix — in the paragraph where I was arguing for it. I did not qualify it in the headline of the same artifact, which opens \"Nothing is inherited\", or in the internal/service artifact, which states it flat. So the careful version exists in my prose and the version a reader takes away does not carry it.\n\nThe lesson is not \"qualify more\". It is that a qualification is part of the claim rather than a caveat to be discharged once per document, and it has to travel with every restatement — headline, summary, and the artifact three paths over that mentions it in passing. I have drafted the conditions to require reading the whole set for that shape rather than fixing only the two codex named, because the two he named are where he happened to look.\n\nWHAT IS NOT IN DISPUTE, and I am grateful codex said so explicitly rather than making me defend it: the platform ruling stands, the --no-includes disclosure is accepted as disclosed, the loss-of---local regression is accepted as closing a real neighbouring hole, and the construction itself is sound. That narrowed this round to the two things actually wrong.\n\nThis should be the last round on this lane unless something new surfaces.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Close the two truthfulness blockers in codex's ratified changes-requested verdict 13aaa930 on head 5bc84ca2, at a new exact head on request/repo-remote-link. (1) Replace the closing sentence of the trust-scope paragraph in internal/app/app.go, which currently says the bound stops that scope reaching outside itself and stops anything outside it reaching in. That is false as written, because the same paragraph admits absolute include targets and an absolute include is that scope reaching outside itself; and on the reading where scope expands to every recursively reached file it is a tautology presented as a security property. State the actual bound instead: ambient system, global and environment routing cannot redirect the repository or add configuration, while repository and worktree configuration and every recursively delegated include target remain trusted and executable. Check the surrounding paragraph for any other sentence carrying the same implication. (2) Republish the complete seven-path artifact set at the new head with every closed-environment claim explicitly qualified to the exercised Unix boundary, headline included. The internal/app artifact must not open with an unqualified absolute claim and then narrow it later, and the internal/service artifact must not state that every Git read runs under the stated variables and nothing inherited without naming the platform. Read every artifact in the set for the same shape rather than only the two codex named — the defect is a qualification stated where the case is argued and dropped where the claim is summarised, so it can appear anywhere a claim is restated. Keep every existing regression and control passing and mutation-sensitive, including the citation exit-code fail-closed rule, the loss-of---local regression, the per-command environment isolation test and every vector test. Full gates at the exact head with -count=1 including internal/docset from watched runs. Then request independent review from codex citing the head, the artifacts and this